{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:18949181 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:18949181 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"18949181","source":"MED","pmid":"18949181","doi":"/s0034-98872008000800004","title":"[Effect of probiotic Saccharomyces boulardii on prevention of antibiotic-associated diarrhea in adult outpatients with amoxicillin treatment].","authorString":"Bravo MV, Bunout D, Leiva L, de la Maza MP, Barrera G, de la Maza J, Hirsch S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bravo MV","lastName":"Bravo","firstName":"María Verónica","initials":"MV","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Instituto de Nutrición y Tecnología de los Alimentos, Universidad de Chile, Santiago, Chile. vbravol@vtr.net"}]}},{"fullName":"Bunout D","lastName":"Bunout","firstName":"Daniel","initials":"D"},{"fullName":"Leiva L","lastName":"Leiva","firstName":"Laura","initials":"L"},{"fullName":"de la Maza MP","lastName":"de la Maza","firstName":"María Pía","initials":"MP"},{"fullName":"Barrera G","lastName":"Barrera","firstName":"Gladys","initials":"G"},{"fullName":"de la Maza J","lastName":"de la Maza","firstName":"Javier","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Hirsch S","lastName":"Hirsch","firstName":"Sandra","initials":"S"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"8","volume":"136","journalIssueId":1573670,"dateOfPublication":"2008 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2008,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Rev Med Chil","title":"Revista medica de Chile","essn":"0717-6163","issn":"0034-9887","nlmid":"0404312","isoabbreviation":"Rev Med Chil"},"printPublicationDate":"2008-08-01"},"pubYear":"2008","pageInfo":"981-988","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Antibiotic-associated diarrhea is one of the most common adverse effects of antimicrobials. Any antimicrobial can potentially produce diarrhea but beta-lactamics have a higher risk. Among these, amoxicillin is widely indicated in ambulatory practice. One of the alternatives suggested to prevent antibiotic-associated diarrhea, is the use of the probiotic Saccharomyces boulardii.<h4>Aim</h4>To evaluate whether the concomitant use of Saccharomyces boulardii and amoxicillin can prevent antibiotic associated diarrhea in ambulatory adults with acute infections diseases, without provoking other adverse effects.<h4>Material and methods</h4>Eighty six adults (aged 15 to 81 years) with acute infectious diseases, excluding those arising in the gastrointestinal tract, that received a prescription of oral amoxicillin for 5 to 10 days, were included. In a controlled randomized, double blind trial, 41 patients were assigned to receive lyophilized Saccharomyces boulardii (500 mg/day) during 12 days, and 45 patients were assigned to placebo for the same period.<h4>Results</h4>Ten percent of patients (9/86) reported acute diarrhea, 9,8% (4/41) in the experimental group and 11.196 (5/45) in the control group (p = 100). No adverse effects were associated to the use of the probiotic.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Saccharomyces boulardii (500 mg/day) did not prevent diarrhea related to amoxicillin.","affiliation":"Instituto de Nutrición y Tecnología de los Alimentos, Universidad de Chile, Santiago, Chile. vbravol@vtr.net","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"spa","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Randomized Controlled Trial","Journal Article","English Abstract"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Saccharomyces"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Diarrhea","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CI","qualifierName":"chemically induced"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PC","qualifierName":"pr